From 3271146b74639d79165168d34b9af08d71bb4ac3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuxiang Ji <kurath0307@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:10:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] doc: RAND(7): a restore from a saved image is not detected;
 reseed after it

The DRBGs reseed on a change of process ID, on the request count and on
the time interval, and have no way to notice that the process or the
machine was restored from a snapshot or a checkpoint: copies restored
from one image continue from the same state until their next scheduled
reseed. Say so in RAND(7) beside the paragraph on automatic reseeding,
and tell applications in such environments to reseed after their own
restore signal, with RAND_poll(3) as the call that does it.

Measured on 3.5.9 and master: two continuations of one QEMU or
Firecracker snapshot, or of one CRIU checkpoint, print identical
RAND_bytes output block for block (32 of 32, 14 of 14) until the primary
DRBG's interval expires; plain fork, _Fork and clone are distinct through
the pid check.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-fable-5-1
Reviewed-by: Tomas Mraz <tomas@openssl.foundation>
Reviewed-by: Paul Dale <paul.dale@oracle.com>
Reviewed-by: Viktor Dukhovni <viktor@openssl.org>
Merge-date: Fri Oct 23 12:51:55 2026
Merged-from: https://github.com/openssl/openssl/pull/33285
(cherry picked from commit f4ada04e0c795e3ff07aa24be79b7240cfbf3388)
---
 doc/man7/RAND.pod | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/doc/man7/RAND.pod b/doc/man7/RAND.pod
index ac46d16e9090..8b837651acd9 100644
--- a/doc/man7/RAND.pod
+++ b/doc/man7/RAND.pod
@@ -35,6 +35,19 @@ In this case the CSPRNG enters an error state and ceases to provide output,
 until it is able to recover from the error by reseeding itself.
 For more details on reseeding and error recovery, see L<EVP_RAND(7)>.
 
+The automatic reseeding reacts to a change of the process ID (a child created
+by fork(2) does not continue its parent's generator), to the number of
+requests made and to the time elapsed since the last reseed; see L<EVP_RAND(7)>.
+It does not detect that the process, or the virtual machine it runs in, has
+been restored from a saved image: a virtual machine restored from a snapshot,
+a process restored from a checkpoint, or a function resumed from an image
+continues from the generator state in the image, and several copies restored
+from one image produce the same output until their next scheduled reseed.
+An application that runs where such a restore can happen should reseed after
+its own restore signal, for example by calling L<RAND_poll(3)>, which reseeds
+the primary DRBG; the public and private DRBGs then reseed from it at their
+next request.
+
 For values that should remain secret, you can use L<RAND_priv_bytes(3)>
 instead.
 This method does not provide 'better' randomness, it uses the same type of
